diff --git a/docs/bot-suspension.md b/docs/bot-suspension.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ef9dbeb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bot-suspension.md
@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+# Bot suspension
+
+Design notes for the automatic stop on misbehaving bots. The rules live in
+`Bot::Conduct` and are not configurable.
+
+## Visible copy
+
+Browser tests look for these exact strings:
+
+- "Suspended" — status on `/bots`
+- "Reactivate" — administrator-only button on `/bots`, with `data-turbo-confirm`
+  "Are you sure you want to reactivate this bot?" (Turbo calls `confirm()`)
+- "Flooding" — suspension reason after five extra post attempts in a cooldown
+- "Failing webhooks" — suspension reason after ten failed deliveries in a row
+
+## Posting
+
+Shared by `POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages` and webhook replies
+(`200` `text/plain` or `text/html`).
+
+1. At most 10 messages in any rolling 10 seconds.
+2. The message that would be the 11th is rejected and starts a 30-second cooldown.
+   It does not itself count as a cooldown attempt. `Retry-After: 30`.
+3. During that cooldown every hit on the posting endpoint is `429` with
+   `Retry-After` set to the seconds left. Webhook replies are not stored, and
+   each one counts as an attempt.
+4. The fifth attempt in that cooldown suspends the bot for "Flooding" and is
+   still answered with `429`. The following request is `403`.
+5. When the cooldown expires without five attempts, the bot can post again.
+   Attempts do not carry over.
+
+A suspended bot is `403` even if the body is empty or the room is unknown.
+Unknown bot codes stay `401`. Empty bodies outside a cooldown stay `422` and
+do not count toward the ten.
+
+## Webhooks
+
+Failed delivery: HTTP status outside `2xx`, timeout, or connection error.
+Ten in a row suspends for "Failing webhooks". Any `2xx` (including `204` and
+`201`) resets the count. An unparseable URL is not a delivery and is not counted.
+
+Suspended bots are skipped when enqueueing `mention` and `joined`, and
+`Bot::WebhookDelivery` refuses to call out if the bot was suspended after the
+job was queued.
+
+## Reactivation
+
+`POST /bots/:bot_id/reactivation` (`Bots::ReactivationsController`).
+Administrators only (`ensure_can_administer`). Clears `suspended_at`,
+`suspension_reason`, the cooldown, recent post timestamps, and the failure
+streak. Memberships, messages, and the bot row are untouched, so the bot still
+counts toward `bots_per_member`.
diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5085dd0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,199 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Bots are members of rooms and post messages over HTTP. They are not a parallel
+identity system: each bot is a `User` (so memberships, avatars, and message
+authorship keep working) plus a `Bot` row that holds the credential and the
+human who created it.
+
+## Managing bots
+
+Any signed-in member can open Account settings (`/account/edit`) and follow
+"Set up chat bots" to `/bots`.
+
+- Members see the bots they created. Administrators see every bot.
+- The list is not paginated. Bot codes are credentials, so the page is the
+  management surface, not a public directory.
+- Creating a bot creates an active user with a random password and a
+  non-routable email (`*@bots.campfire.invalid`). People still join every open
+  room. Bots do not, unless "Add to all open rooms" is on. Closed rooms and
+  pings include bots in the existing people pickers.
+- Deleting a bot destroys the credential, removes memberships, and deactivates
+  the user. Messages already posted stay, with the bot's name as the author.
+  A deleted bot cannot post (`401`).
+
+Bots are excluded from the account people list, direct-room placeholders, and
+session-transfer links so they cannot be promoted, banned, or signed in as a
+person. Room membership is unchanged: add a bot the same way you add a person.
+
+## Bots per member
+
+Administrators cap how many bots each member can create from Account settings
+(`/account/edit`). The control is labelled "Bots per member" and defaults to 5.
+It is stored on `accounts.settings` as `bots_per_member` (`has_json`, integer).
+No migration is required; a missing value reads as 5.
+
+- A member can have at most that many bots. The count is existing `bots` rows
+  they created (`creator_id`). Deleting a bot destroys the row, so deleted bots
+  do not count.
+- Administrators are not limited, whatever the number is.
+- At the limit, `/bots` does not offer "Add a chat bot" and tells the member
+  they have reached the limit. `GET /bots/new` and `POST /bots` redirect back
+  to that page without creating a bot. `Bot` validates on create, so any other
+  attempt (including `Bot.create`) is refused too.
+- Lowering the limit never removes existing bots and does not block editing or
+  deleting them. It only blocks new ones.
+- Zero is allowed and stops members from creating bots. Negative or blank
+  values are rejected and not saved.
+
+## Posting
+
+```
+curl -d "Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅" \
+  https://chat.example.com/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages
+```
+
+The raw request body is the message body. There is no JSON envelope and no
+field name. The endpoint does not use a session or a CSRF token; the bot code
+in the path is the credential. Treat that URL like a password (HTTPS only).
+
+| Status | When |
+| --- | --- |
+| `201 Created` | Message stored. `Location: /messages/:message_id` |
+| `422 Unprocessable Content` | Empty body and no attachment |
+| `404 Not Found` | Unknown room id, or the bot is not a member of that room |
+| `401 Unauthorized` | Unknown bot code, or the bot has been deleted or deactivated |
+| `429 Too Many Requests` | The bot is in a posting cooldown. `Retry-After` is the seconds left. |
+| `403 Forbidden` | The bot is suspended. An administrator has to reactivate it. |
+
+A non-text content type (for example `image/png`) is stored as an attachment
+instead of a message body, so a bot can post a file without a form field name.
+Multipart uploads with a file part are accepted the same way.
+
+The message is created as the bot user, then broadcast and pushed through the
+same `Message` callbacks a human post uses.
+
+## Webhooks
+
+A bot may have an optional webhook URL, set on the same form used to create or
+edit the bot (`/bots/new` and `/bots/:id/edit`). The field is labelled
+"Webhook URL". Saving a blank value deletes the bot's `Webhook` row; it is not
+stored as an empty string.
+
+The webhook belongs to the bot's user (`webhooks.user_id`). Bots are users, so
+memberships, mentions, and authorship stay on the existing path.
+
+The same form has an "Add to all open rooms" switch. It is off by default, on
+both `/bots/new` and `/bots/:id/edit`. It is a real checkbox (styled as a
+switch).
+
+- Off: the bot joins no open room by itself. Someone can still add it to a
+  closed room, an open room, or a direct conversation the same way they add a
+  person.
+- On: the bot becomes a member of every open room that already exists, and of
+  every open room created afterwards (including a closed room that is later
+  opened). Direct conversations and closed rooms are unchanged.
+- Turning the switch off does not remove memberships the bot already has. It
+  only stops automatic joins from then on.
+- The open-room people list shows who actually has access: every person, bots
+  with the switch on, and anyone already a member. A bot left off the switch
+  is not listed there.
+
+### Joined callback
+
+Whenever a bot is added to a room — by the switch, because a new open room
+appeared while the switch was on, or because a person granted membership —
+its webhook receives one `joined` event for that room. Repeating a grant for
+a room the bot is already in does not send another event. Removing the bot
+and adding it again does, because that is a new membership.
+
+Direct conversations never send `joined`. A bot with no webhook is still added;
+there is just no HTTP call.
+
+The job is `Bot::DeliverJoinedWebhookJob`. It is enqueued after the membership
+insert commits, and it POSTs `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+- `room.reply_to` is the bot's posting URL for that room (relative), the same
+  path a mention uses.
+- There is no `author` and no `message`. The bot has not been spoken to yet.
+- A `200` `text/plain` or `text/html` body is posted into that room as the
+  bot's message, the same way a mention reply is. `204` and anything else are
+  ignored. That is how a bot says hello.
+
+### Mention callback
+
+Bots show up in the @-mention autocomplete the same way people do, because they
+are room members. When a message mentions a bot that has a webhook, Campfire
+enqueues `Bot::DeliverWebhookJob`. The HTTP call is never made in the request
+that stored the message.
+
+The job POSTs `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div>… full rich-text body …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+- `event` is `mention` here. `joined` is the other event; see above.
+- `message.body.text` has that bot's own @-mention removed. Other mentions stay.
+- `message.body.html` is the full Action Text body, mentions included.
+- `room.reply_to` is the bot's posting URL for that room (relative).
+- `message.link` scrolls the room to the message (relative).
+- A bot is not notified about its own messages, so a reply cannot loop.
+
+### Answering
+
+`mention` and `joined` are answered the same way. The response to the webhook
+POST is the reply; the bot does not have to call `reply_to` itself.
+
+| Response | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| `200` `text/plain` | Body is escaped and posted as the bot's message |
+| `200` `text/html` | Body is posted as the bot's rich-text message |
+| `204` | Nothing is posted |
+| anything else | Ignored |
+
+Plain-text replies use the same line wrapping as the posting endpoint, so the
+text round-trips. The reply is broadcast on the room's message stream.
+
+## Misbehaving bots
+
+The limits are fixed in `Bot::Conduct`. They are not account settings.
+
+### Flooding
+
+A bot may post at most 10 messages in any 10 seconds. Posts through the API and webhook replies share that window. The message that would exceed it is not stored and starts a 30-second cooldown.
+
+During the cooldown the posting endpoint responds with `429 Too Many Requests` and a `Retry-After` header (seconds left), including requests that would otherwise be `404` or `422`. Webhook deliveries still run, but their replies are not posted. Five further attempts during that same cooldown suspend the bot. Those five attempts are still `429`; the next attempt is `403 Forbidden`.
+
+### Failing webhooks
+
+A failed delivery is any response other than `2xx`, a timeout, or a connection error. Ten failed deliveries in a row suspend the bot. Any `2xx` resets the streak.
+
+### Suspended
+
+A suspended bot:
+
+- gets `403 Forbidden` from the posting endpoint
+- receives no webhooks (`mention` and `joined` are not enqueued, and a job already queued does not call out)
+- keeps its rooms and messages, stays an active user, and still counts toward its owner's bot limit
+- stays on `/bots` and shows as "Suspended", with the reason: "Flooding" or "Failing webhooks"
+
+Only an administrator can bring it back, from the bots page: a "Reactivate" button that asks `confirm()` via `data-turbo-confirm`. Reactivation clears the cooldown, the recent-post window, and the webhook failure streak.
